node_modules/
build/
dist/
coverage/
.DS_Store
*.log
.env*
!.env.example

# OS and editor droppings. `.DS_Store` above was the only one covered, which is
# the wrong half for this project: development happens on Windows, where
# Explorer writes `Thumbs.db` into any folder whose images it has previewed and
# `desktop.ini` into any folder given a custom icon. Neither has ever been
# committed here -- these are a backstop, in the same spirit as the credential
# patterns further down, not a response to an incident.
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*.swo
.idea/

# Claude Code's personal settings. `.claude/settings.json` beside it IS committed
# and holds the shared allowlist for the commands this project documents -- that
# split is the tool's own convention, and it was the wrong way round here until
# 2026-08-30: the *personal* file was the tracked one, so any contributor's own
# permission edits would have collided with the owner's in git.
#
# The tracked copy had also fossilised. Five of its twelve entries were one-off
# probes from long-finished debugging sessions -- one allowed reading App.tsx
# around four specific line numbers, another fetched /@vite/client to grep it for
# the word "process". They could never fire again and meant nothing to anyone
# else. The seven that survive are the documented commands: npm run *, npm test,
# npm ls *, npx tsc *, npx vitest *, New-Item *, Get-Process *.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Firebase's official agent skill packs. Untracked on 2026-08-30, ahead of making
# this repository public, and kept on disk so they still work locally.
#
# Two reasons, and the first is the one that decided it. They are 44 markdown
# files of Google's own documentation -- 75% of every .md file this repo tracked,
# ~192 KB -- carrying no LICENSE, no NOTICE, no copyright line and no statement
# of where they came from. Publishing them inside an Apache-2.0 repository would
# redistribute someone else's writing with nothing telling a reader it is not
# ours. The fonts in src/fonts/ set the standard the same week: OFL texts
# shipped, copyright recorded per family. These did not meet it.
#
# Second, CONTRIBUTING.md's rule already excluded them: a file must be used by
# code that actually ships or actually runs. Nothing does. `git grep .agents`
# over anything but markdown returns nothing -- no build step, no script, no CI
# job reads them; they are instructions for one particular AI assistant.
#
# Not deleted, because they are genuinely useful when working on firestore.rules
# or the auth flow. Anyone who wants them can install the official packs.
.agents/

# Full-resolution logo masters. Untracked on 2026-08-29: the 2048px white mark
# alone was 2.5 MB and 50% of the repository, cloned in full by everyone who has
# ever cloned it, to serve a 256px derivative that is committed beside it in
# `public/`. Nothing in the build reads them, and `assets/source/og-card.html`
# renders from `../../public/logo.png`, not from these — so the artwork the
# project actually ships is unaffected.
#
# They are design originals and belong wherever the rest of the design source
# lives. The last committed copies are recoverable — `git log --diff-filter=D
# --name-only` finds the path, `git show <commit>~1:<path>` gets the bytes — but
# once a NEW master is produced it will exist only on whatever machine made it.
# That is the trade this rule accepts; see the note in docs/notes/styling.md.
#
# `og-card.html` beside them stays tracked on purpose: it is the recipe for
# regenerating public/og-card.png, not artwork.
assets/source/*.png

# `.vscode/` was the one editor directory NOT covered here, and it was tracked:
# `.vscode/settings.json` held `{"workbench.startupEditor":"readme"}` -- 42 bytes
# of one person's window preference, committed and about to be published.
# CONTRIBUTING.md already said never to commit personal editor config; `.idea/`
# above was ignored while its VS Code equivalent was not, which is exactly the
# half-covered shape the OS-droppings comment describes. `launch.json` lived here
# too and was removed earlier.
#
# If a genuinely shared setting is ever needed -- a formatter or a recommended
# extension the whole project depends on -- unignore that ONE file by path
# (`!.vscode/extensions.json`) rather than the directory, so personal preferences
# cannot ride in beside it.
.vscode/

# Backup and scratch copies. The failure this prevents is `git add .` after an
# editor or a merge has left `App.tsx.orig` next to `App.tsx` -- a 178 KB near
# duplicate of the largest file in the repo, committed by accident and then
# read by someone as if it were real. Archives are here for the same reason: a
# .zip in a source tree is almost always a download nobody meant to keep.
*.bak
*.old
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.rar
*.7z

# Editor state, not project data: a single ~22 MB .pbtxt that nothing reads and
# nothing builds from. It matched no pattern here before, so a first `git add .`
# would have committed it and put 22 MB into every clone forever.
.antigravity/

# Credentials pasted into the working tree by mistake. A Gemini key was once
# committed here via envPrefix and revoked by Google's secret scanner; a plain
# key.txt in the repo root is the same accident with fewer steps. These patterns
# are a backstop, not permission — keys belong in the app's Settings dialog,
# which stores them in sessionStorage and never on disk.
key.txt
*.key
*apikey*
*api-key*
secrets.json

# Firebase CLI/emulator working files. `firestore-debug.log` and
# `firebase-debug.log` are already covered by *.log above; `.firebase/` holds
# deploy caches and emulator exports and is not.
#
# `.firebaserc` is deliberately NOT ignored — it names the deploy target
# (forma-201ba) and holds no credentials. Ignoring it is how a fresh clone or a
# CI run ends up deploying to whatever project the CLI last happened to select.
.firebase/

# .NET build output from tools/RepxDesigner. A bare `bin/` would be too broad in
# a JS repo, so both are scoped to tools/. Release is ~146 MB, because the
# DevExpress assemblies are copied local so the tool runs standalone.
tools/**/bin/
tools/**/obj/

# A `_not_required/` quarantine folder existed here from 2026-08-28 to
# 2026-08-29, with its own ignore rules, a CI check and a pre-commit rule. It was
# retired: git history is the recovery mechanism this project actually uses, and
# a second one that had to be enforced by three separate mechanisms was not
# earning its keep. Removals now go through `git rm` and are recovered with
# `git show <commit>~1:<path>` — the comments left at each removal site name the
# commit. Do not reintroduce the folder without also reintroducing what enforced
# it; a quarantine nothing polices is just a directory of files people forget.

# RepxProbe writes <input>.resaved beside whatever it inspects.
*.repx.resaved
